The size and proportions of the ringlet butterfly are relatively small compared to other butterfly species, with a wingspan ranging from 20-30 mm. This compact size allows it to navigate through dense vegetation and flower clusters easily.

Behaviorally, ringlet butterflies are known for their preference for feeding on nectar from flowers, particularly those with tubular shapes like milkweed. They are also known to perch on stems and leaves while feeding, which can be observed in the provided images.

One unique identifying trait of the ringlet butterfly is its ability to mimic the appearance of dead leaves, which helps it blend into its environment and avoid detection by predators. This camouflage feature is evident in the images, where the butterfly's wings resemble the texture and shape of a dried leaf.

